    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense. what about the art though? if the pieces are stored in a freeport or something like that to avoid certain regulations, does the HMRC still look at those as part of the estate? i'm worried about there being some kind of hidden liability we aren't seeing.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Yes, HMRC considers art stored in freeports part of the estate if the deceased had legal ownership or control over the assets at the time of death; freeport storage does not shield these items from inheritance tax. The tax base is determined by the person's ownership, not where the physical items are kept, so the value of the artwork is still counted toward the taxable threshold. To avoid hidden liabilities, you should review the current trust deed to ensure the assets are not mistakenly excluded and consider a Will with specific instructions for their disposal. Consulting a specialist in art taxation is also wise to quantify the correct market value before the tax return is due.",
